e hormone of love they call  it the cuddle hormone  the love nursing mothers and  it promotes new neural connections new  growth of  neurons in new pathways  what happens in these studies when with  the studies with people  is there like a placebo group where do  people know whether they're getting  the mdma or not because that seems like  it would be a relevant question in terms  of measuring the impact right  yeah that's a great question um my  dissertation at the kennedy school of  government  uh where i got my master's in phd was on  the regulation of the medical use of  psychedelics and one of the main things  i looked at was how do you do a  double-blind  study with a drug like a psychedelic  that's pretty obvious if you've taken it  it's not like you're taking different  pills and you don't notice  automatically what they do these are  very powerful drugs that change your  consciousness  and you usually can tell if that's  happening sure so  a lot of my dissertation was about how  to solve this problem and  i thought i had the solution the  solution i thought was going to be  therapy with low dose mdma versus  therapy with full dose mdma  and the challenge was to find the dose  of mdma that was  high enough that enough people would  notice it  and would not be certain was it the low  dose or the high dose  but that it wasn't so high that you  started getting all the therapeutic  potentials of mdma  and this is what we explored in phase  two  so maps i started in 1986 we started our  first phase one  safety study in 1992 and in 1999 and  2000 we started the phase two studies  and that took us till 2016.  but what we discovered and this is i  guess you could say the  the beauty and humility of scientific  research  is that you discover stuff that you  didn't realize was  actually going on or you discover that  you were wrong so what i discovered  through these studies and we tested uh  inactive placebo  with therapy 25 milligrams 30 milligrams  40 milligrams 50 milligrams 75  milligrams 100 125 and 150  and what we showed is that the  therapeutic dose  was lower than we thought 75 milligrams  was very  effective but that the lower doses  they did cause a certain amount of  inaccurate guesses  but what they did is they had an  anti-therapeutic effect  meaning that everybody still got better  who got these lower therapy plus lower  doses but  when we looked at the data the people  that had therapy without mdma did better  than the people that had therapy with  low-dose mdma  and the best way to describe it it's  like if you're in an airplane and you're  about to take off sometimes there's  turbulence at the beginning  and then you get above the clouds and  then it's smooth sailing  so the low doses of mdma were like this  turbulence they  people were focused on their trauma they  were activated a little bit but the fear  reduction properties of mdma  weren't fully activated so right made  people uncomfortable  so when we went to the fda to design  phase three  um i opened it up by saying that there's  a funny quote by a former president of  harvard  and what he said was never forget  there's always a harvard man on the  wrong side of  every issue and so i said  in this case it's me my dissertation  which fortunately i managed to get  my theory was wrong and that there is no  solution to the double-blind problem  and so he said to the fda we can give  you the blinding but you're going to  make it  easier for us to find a difference  between the two groups and the real  question is  if you can do this work with therapy  without adding a drug  why add the drug so rather than adding a  drug that gives a little bit of  blinding it's more important that we  test the therapy by itself where it's  the most effective  and so the fda brought in what we like  to joke as the old wise man of the fda  his name is bob temple  and he's been there since 1972 and he  was the office of science policy and he  came and he said  there is he agreed there is no solution  to the double-blind problem  and he said that there there are other  ways to try to  reduce experiment or bias the double  blind is really to make it so that  you know you don't get biased results  that you don't find what you want to  find  right and so what what they first off  said is what's called random assignment  that means  everybody is similarly motivated and  then they get randomized either to the  control group or the  experimental group the other thing they  said is that  the independent raters who evaluate  the outcomes that you can't have the  therapist say oh did this person get  better or not or even the person  themselves  to to do it that you need independent  raters  and this is done through an hour-long  interview with the patient about all of  their symptoms  and so we have these independent raiders  in a very robust system we have over 20  of them trained by the boston veterans  administration  where they develop the measure they do  it by random assignment  meaning it's all telemedicine so they  don't know  the patient where they are in the  treatment process  they don't know if they've got mdma or  the placebo so  based on that the fda said that they  would agree with us that we would do  therapy without mdma versus therapy with  mdma  even still there's about 10 of the  people that are confused  about what they got which is pretty  astonishing but sometimes people  resist the emotional effects of mdma  because they're so scared of what's  going on they have kind of a muted  effect  so there's a little bit of confusion but  we also negotiated with the european  medicines agency we're trying to  negotiate all over the world to  globalize mdma therapy  and so the european medicines agency  went through the same analysis and they  also agreed with the fda  that it's therapy without mdma versus  therapy  with mdma and therapy with inactive  placebo  and we're also doing research in israel  and canada  we've done some in b